From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrew Williams <awillia@chromium.org>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 09:25:45 -0700
Subject: GPU: Check graphics reset status before delivering readback results

On Mali GPUs, a device reset that occurs while a readback is blocked
can release the wait with no GL error while the destination was never
written by the GPU. The robustness reset status is already latched by
the time the blocked call returns, but the decoders only poll it at
the next MakeCurrent, one command too late, so unwritten destination
bytes are delivered to the client with a success result.

Poll the sticky reset status at the readback delivery points -- the
synchronous ReadPixels result publish and the async readback
completion on both command decoders, and the raster/shared-image
readback publishes (RasterDecoder ARGB and YUV, and the passthrough
ReadbackARGBImagePixels path) -- and refuse delivery on a non-clean
status: scrub the shared-memory destination, leave the result
unsuccessful, and lose the context. Per OpenGL ES 3.2 sec. 2.3.2,
commands on a context after a graphics reset generate CONTEXT_LOST
and do not have side effects such as writing readback results; this
applies that behavior at every point where readback bytes become
client-visible. It is the same CheckResetStatus() poll the decoders
already trust at MakeCurrent, moved to delivery.

Gated behind the check_graphics_reset_status_after_readback
workaround on Android ARM Mali GPUs. Unit tests coverage added to
better ensure no major behavioral changes, and the patch was tested
against repro steps from each of the bugs linked below to ensure
they fix the issue.

Bug: 552550589, 550525698, 550996500, 555394151, 558111285
Change-Id: I311e77d251667a3f7ddb21f185512cb6e00f358f
Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/chromium/src/+/8475151
Reviewed-by: Kenneth Russell <kbr@chromium.org>
Commit-Queue: Andrew Williams <awillia@chromium.org>
Cr-Commit-Position: refs/heads/main@{#1707168}

